why_included: >
  What an agent needs from DIFF, and the load-bearing word is "actually changed": byte diffs
  overstate and the wire can rename events without renaming meaning. Transferable tool demands:
  semantic-grain diff that folds reflow/re-wrapping to zero change ("a diff tool that reports
  reflow as change would train agents to ignore it"); element-aligned-by-identity + attr-aligned-
  by-name reporting with zero positional noise; stacked-attribute diffs as ordered value lists
  (append vs remove vs reorder are distinct edit kinds — reporting append as wholesale
  replacement is "semantically equal, provenance-hostile"); move-recognition vs delete-plus-add;
  and the parser-version differential ("same bytes, two parser versions, event streams compared")
  promoted from an internal CI method (CONSUMERS.md) to a first-class tool op, as the guard
  against the Norway-problem class — "parser upgrades must be auditable at the meaning grain,
  not the byte grain." The diff/patch duality thesis is stated up front: a diff SHOULD be
  expressible as the patch that would produce it.
---

; 02-diffing.scenarios.udon — a day in the life, midday
; Diff journeys for the future udon diff tool (the read half of the patch
; language: a diff SHOULD be expressible as the patch that would produce
; it). Conventions: ../README.md. Path syntax provisional (see 01).

|feature[diffing].day-in-the-life
  :date <2026-07-21>
  :tool udon-diff
  :path-syntax provisional-2026-07
  :corpus ../corpus/

  Yesterday evening the upstream stewards landed edits to the lexicon and
  the ordinum. Before touching anything, the day's agents ask what
  actually changed — and "actually" is the load-bearing word: byte diffs
  overstate, and the wire can rename events without renaming meaning.

  |scenario[semantic-vs-textual]
    :at <11:05> :agent lexicographer
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    Yesterday's commit reflowed the |term[promise] definition paragraph
    to a narrower width. git shows nine changed lines.

    |given the lexicon at HEAD~1 and at HEAD, where the only change is
      prose re-wrapping inside |term[promise]
    |when
      |diff :from HEAD~1 :to HEAD :grain semantic
    |then
      |expect :changes 0
      Line-grain Text events differ, but the tool folds text-reducible
      runs before comparing (the same folding the compliance harness
      already does — the wire's granularity non-guarantee cuts both
      ways). A diff tool that reports reflow as change would train
      agents to ignore it.

  |scenario[status-flip]
    :at <11:20> :agent lexicographer
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    |given the lexicon at HEAD, and a working copy where
      |term[regime-change] has :status settled, a sharpened first
      paragraph, and a new relation edge
    |when
      |diff :from HEAD :to working :grain semantic
    |then the report is element-aligned by (type, key) and attr-aligned
      by name
      !:text:
        ~ |term[regime-change]
            :status  open -> settled
            ~ prose  (1 paragraph rewritten)
            + |rel :to phase-gate :kind certified-at
      |expect :changes 3 :aligned-by identity
      No positional noise: terms above and below the edit do not appear
      in the report at all.

  |scenario[trait-append-is-not-replacement]
    :at <11:30> :agent ordinator
    :file corpus/terrestris.ordinum.udon

    Promise classes are stacked $traits assignments. The working copy
    reclassifies ore-and-soil from .state to .state.regime.

    |given the ordinum at HEAD and a working copy where
      promise[ore-and-soil] carries traits [state regime]
    |when
      |diff :from HEAD :to working :grain semantic
    |then
      |expect :change "+ $traits regime (appended after state)"
      Stacked attributes diff as ordered value lists — append, remove,
      reorder are each their own edit kind. Reporting this as
      "traits: [state] -> [state regime]" (wholesale replacement) would
      round-trip into a patch that rewrites the identity line instead of
      appending one assignment — semantically equal, provenance-hostile.

  |scenario[move-not-rewrite]
    :at <11:45> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    A working copy moves |process[drift-repair] from cluster[coherence]
    into cluster[integration] — eleven lines relocate.

    |given the map at HEAD and that working copy
    |when
      |diff :from HEAD :to working :grain semantic
    |then
      |expect :change "> |process[drift-repair] moved: |cluster[coherence] -> |cluster[integration] (content unchanged)"
      Identity-keyed alignment recognizes the move; a delete-plus-add
      report would invite a reviewer to re-read eleven unchanged lines.
      Position within the new parent is part of the move record (here:
      last child).

  |scenario[wire-rename-vs-meaning-change]
    :at <12:00> :agent steward
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    The differential method from CONSUMERS.md, as a first-class tool op:
    same bytes, two parser versions, event streams compared.

    |given the workspace file unchanged, parsed by the core-v0.8.0 parser
      and by the current 0.9 parser
    |when
      |diff :from parser@core-v0.8.0 :to parser@current :grain semantic
    |then pure wire renames (BareValue -> Text for multi-word blobs) fold
      to zero reported changes, but re-owned text is a MEANING change and
      must surface loudly
      !:text:
        ~ |realization[r-0042-1]:outcome
          0.8: "udon-core"           (rest stranded as element prose)
          0.9: "udon-core and udon-cli verified available and reserved"
          class: ownership change (blob row-1) — toward author intent
      |expect :meaning-changes 1 :wire-renames folded
      This is the guard against the Norway-problem class: parser upgrades
      must be auditable at the meaning grain, not the byte grain.
